cause the glow from the  star gets a tiny bit dimmer each time a  planet  crosses in front of its class from this  data they could estimate the mass and  density of these planets in total the  Spitzberg spacecraft monitored the solar  system for about 500 hours traditionally  in the 20th century exoplanets were  originally found by the wobble that they  induced in a star as their gravitational  pull affected it this method only just  allowed us to know that these planets  existed for a visual example check out  the wobble of the star on the left in  this artist representation nowadays in  the 21st century we can analyze in much  more detail here's how it's done when  light from a star passes through the  atmospheric horizon of a planet and  travels trillions of kilometres to us  that light is affected by gases in the  alien planet's atmosphere the different  wavelengths of light are absorbed by  different gases by analyzing the light  spectrum we can actually gain insight  into the chemical composition and likely  makeup of that atmosphere that's not bad  for being trillions of kilometres away  now the funny thing about all of this is  that the script the telescope which has  launched back in 2003 was never supposed  to be operational for this long the  craft wasn't even planned to do any of  this so how does this telescope actually  communicate back with scientists on  earth the telescope actually follows the  orbit of the earth but are just slightly  slower speed as the craft gets further  away from us  it's an turn after point at increasingly  higher angles to communicate with us  unfortunately at the same time this  higher angle of tilt results in less  sunlight reaching the solar panels of  the craft this means that there's less  powers of the telescope so eventually  we're going to lose communication with  the craft because it would lose power  completely but this isn't the end of  things like in 2018 NASA will be  launching the James Webb telescope this  is a massive leap forward in all  previous effort and from this telescope  will actually be able to see images of  the distant planet  part of the Spitzer's extended mission  is to scout out new areas for the James  Webb telescope to look at in more detail  it's kind of like passing on the baton  between two telescopes one final point  of interest that you might like to know  is that believe it or not  x-ray planet hunting is actually very  recent the first exoplanet was  discovered in 1995 and its name was 51  pegasi B this planet was a hot unkind  place very close to its parent star and  about half the size of Jupiter since  then thousands of exoplanets have been  found this is really a testament to how  smart was gotten over the past quarter  century but it's more a measure of how  technology has improved allowing us to  be able to detect more planets it  doesn't stop there though recent  statistical estimates say that there's  at least one planet around every star in  our galaxy that's something like a truly  an exoplanet in our galaxy alone  many of these are earth sized lifetime  is fascinating all in all it kind of  makes you feel small in a way pretty  much every part of world history every  person you've ever heard about or known  and all things relative to us throughout  time have just happened on this tiny  planet which is a microscopic part of a  much bigger picture anyway that was just  a quick video highlighting NASA's latest  discovery I hope you found that  interesting and learn something from  that if you've just stumbled across this  channel feel free to subscribe this has  been - go go you're been watching cold  fusion thanks for watching and I'll  catch you again soon for the next video  Cheers  have a good one cold fusion  it's me thinking  [Music]  you
 

